---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfEditFillForm from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfEditFillForm.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="fill-pdf-form">
  <PdfEditFillForm client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>What a "fillable PDF" really is</h2>
      <p>
        A fillable PDF has an <strong>AcroForm</strong>: a dictionary of named fields, each with a type, a
        value and one or more widgets (rectangles on specific pages where the field is drawn). When you
        click a box in a PDF reader, you click a widget. When you type, the value of the field changes and
        the reader draws the appearance of the widget again so you can see it.
      </p>
      <p>
        This tool reads that structure directly. It lists each field with its name, type and page, draws an
        input over each widget so you can fill the form on the page, and writes the values back into the
        same field objects. Nothing is approximated and no text is typed on top: the output is a correctly
        filled AcroForm.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Editable or flattened: pick deliberately</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[620px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">Editable</th>
              <th scope="col">Flattened</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Fields still exist</td>
              <td>Yes: anyone can change an answer</td>
              <td>No: the answers are page content</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Looks identical in every reader</td>
              <td>Usually, sometimes not</td>
              <td>Always</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Prints reliably</td>
              <td>Depends on the reader's settings</td>
              <td>Always</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Survives an upload portal that rasterises</td>
              <td>Risky</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Use it when</td>
              <td>Others must still complete the form</td>
              <td>You send back the finished copy</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The most common failure (you fill a form, email it, and the recipient sees empty boxes) almost always
        comes from an editable file opened in a reader that does not regenerate field appearances. A
        flattened file cannot have this problem.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Field types you will meet</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Text: one line or several, sometimes with a maximum length or a comb layout of fixed character cells.</li>
        <li>Checkbox: on or off. The form author defines the name of the "on" value, so some checkboxes look ticked in one reader and blank in another.</li>
        <li>Radio group: one field with several widgets, one per option; only one can be selected.</li>
        <li>Dropdown: one choice from a list. Some are editable, so you can type a value that is not in the list.</li>
        <li>List: like a dropdown, but with multiple selection.</li>
        <li>Signature field: a placeholder for a cryptographic signature. This tool leaves these empty, because it does not issue certificates. To place a visual signature, use <a href="/sign-pdf">Sign PDF</a>.</li>
        <li>Read-only: locked by the author, usually a reference number or a calculated total. Shown greyed out and never changed.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
      <h3>"No form fields in this PDF"</h3>
      <p>
        The file is flat. It can be a scan, a design exported without form fields, or a form that the sender
        already flattened. Use <a href="/add-text-to-pdf">Add Text to PDF</a> to type onto the page; that
        tool is made for this case.
      </p>
      <h3>"This is an XFA form"</h3>
      <p>
        XFA is the XML forms architecture of Adobe. The pages you see are often only a placeholder that asks
        you to open the file in a desktop reader, and the real layout is in an XML payload. No browser can
        fill one correctly. Open it in a desktop PDF reader that supports XFA, or ask the issuer for a
        standard AcroForm version; most departments have one.
      </p>
      <h3>A field looks empty after saving</h3>
      <p>
        Some producers create fields with no appearance stream. This tool generates appearances with
        Helvetica on save, which handles the common cases, but a field whose default appearance names an
        unusual font can still look blank in a strict reader. Save the flattened version: the text is then
        painted into the page and is always visible.
      </p>
      <h3>The on-page inputs are in the wrong place</h3>
      <p>
        The tool reads widget rectangles from the file and maps them through the rotation and crop box of the
        page, so this should be rare. A badly generated form can have widgets that do not match its visible
        boxes. Hide the on-page inputs and use the full field list below, which uses field names, not
        coordinates.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why the privacy point is not decorative here</h2>
      <p>
        PDF forms ask for your address, date of birth, salary, bank details, national insurance or social
        security number, medical history and immigration status. For most people this is the most valuable
        data they ever type into a web page, and online form fillers usually send all of it, with the
        document, to a server.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here nothing is autosaved, stored or transmitted. Your answers exist in the memory of this tab while
        you type, and go into a file when you press a build button. If you refresh the page, they are gone,
        which is correct for a tax return on a shared computer.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
